WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:00.560
Speaker 1: Foreign.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.600
Speaker 2: Hello and God bless you.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.560
Speaker 2: My name is Pastor Bright.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.960
Speaker 2: I'm the resident pastor of CCI Toronto.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.280
Speaker 2: If you're watching this, it means you are participating in the celebration.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.360
Speaker 2: Church discipleship.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:26.650
Speaker 2: Experience.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:26.960
Speaker 2: Experience.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.800
Speaker 2: And I want to welcome you.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.960
Speaker 2: We're taking on a very special topic today, and I'm not doing it alone.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.720
Speaker 2: I have with me.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:36.280
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.280
Speaker 1: Hi, everybody.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.800
Speaker 1: I'm Pastor Inyulua Bankoli, Excited to be talking about this very special topic today.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.880
Speaker 1: And we're starting this series on apologetics.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:50.800
Speaker 1: Apologetics.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.850
Speaker 1: And if you've been wondering what apologetics is, you're in the best place and we're going to talk about it.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:09.530
Speaker 1: And one of the reasons why we are looking at this topic is because regardless of where you stay or where you reside in the world, being able to understand what you believe, why you believe what you believe is so crucial.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.770
Speaker 1: It's so crucial to the fundamentals and your growth as a Christian.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:20.560
Speaker 2: So, as a matter of fact, the very first time I heard the word apologetics, I was racking my head.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.320
Speaker 2: I'm like, what do you mean I have to apologize for what I believe?

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.280
Speaker 2: I mean, I thought were all, you know, sold out to Jesus and all that.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.240
Speaker 2: I never really understood what it means.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:37.520
Speaker 2: But just to help you grasp it a bit better, apologetics comes from a Greek word, apologia.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:38.960
Speaker 2: Apologia.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.040
Speaker 2: So A P O L O G, I, A.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.840
Speaker 2: And the word simply means defense.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.400
Speaker 2: So it means defense and not apology.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:48.440
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.200
Speaker 2: You're not apologizing for what you believe.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.280
Speaker 2: You're giving a defense.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.200
Speaker 2: You're giving a reasoned statement or an argument.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.400
Speaker 2: Think of it this way.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.720
Speaker 2: You find yourself in a court of law.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:11.090
Speaker 2: There's an accusation against you, and you're presenting evidence, facts to help convince beyond reasonable doubt for why you are, where you are, why you believe, what you believe, etcetera, and there are examples in the Bible.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.650
Speaker 2: You can read Acts 22:11.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.730
Speaker 2: Paul was on missionary journeys.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:22.530
Speaker 2: He was falsely accused and he had to stand before the officials in trial.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.850
Speaker 2: And so he presented some arguments.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:28.730
Speaker 2: You see the thing happening again in Acts 25 and verse 16.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.130
Speaker 2: So it was a common thing.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:30.690
Speaker 2: Right?

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:36.270
Speaker 2: So when you hear apologetics, you're not apologizing, you are giving a defense.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.670
Speaker 2: So if you wanted to give it a formal definition, do you have a formal definition?

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:41.350
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.150
Speaker 1: So this is a good time to write and take notes.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:55.950
Speaker 1: So apologetics is the religious discipline of defending religious doctrines through systematic argumentation and discourse.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:01.310
Speaker 1: And you see, we see that exemplified even with the early Christians and the early church Right.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.310
Speaker 1: The Berean Church.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.830
Speaker 1: They went and they studied the Church fathers.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:10.790
Speaker 1: We see them taking their time to be able to systematically defend the faith that we believe.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:21.030
Speaker 1: So when it comes to Christian apologetics, what we're basically doing is we are providing a logical, systematic argument for why we believe what we believe as Christians,.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.950
Speaker 2: If so, therefore, why.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.110
Speaker 2: You know, exactly, stuff like that.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:30.990
Speaker 2: And so if I was to ask you, why should we be interested in apologetics?

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.590
Speaker 2: Because some people think it is for the academia.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:42.310
Speaker 2: It's for the scholars, Bible scholars who debate morning till evening and, you know, who don't have nine to fives and all that.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:49.110
Speaker 2: But if I was to give you one reason why you should be interested in apologetics, I would read from first Peter, chapter three and verse 15.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:56.750
Speaker 2: It's a very popular scripture, and I think this serves as the foundation for our journey into apologetics.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:11.390
Speaker 2: It says, sanctify the Lord God in your hearts and always be ready to give a defense to everyone who asks you, not just in the academia, not just when you're presenting a thesis.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:24.020
Speaker 2: He said to give a defense to everyone on the streets, in your office, at home, for the everyone who asks you a reason for the hope that is in you, with meekness and fear.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.460
Speaker 2: So why should I be interested in apologetics?

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.700
Speaker 2: The Bible instructs me to be interested.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:31.140
Speaker 2: The Bible says so.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.380
Speaker 2: The Lord demands it.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:37.380
Speaker 2: He says, sanctify the Lord in your heart and be ready to give a defense.

00:04:38.339 --> 00:04:38.900
Speaker 2: Do you.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.580
Speaker 2: Can you think of any other reason?

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:45.780
Speaker 1: Yeah, there's one that comes to mind, and it's the fact that your faith is reasonable.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:56.890
Speaker 1: You know, we live in a culture where people see Christianity as a faith that is blind, or you just are using it as an excuse to hold on to something.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:00.090
Speaker 1: Think praying to, you know, the big man up there.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:08.730
Speaker 1: But we see in Scripture, we see in the early church that your faith is reasonable.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:12.410
Speaker 1: You know, a good example is the story of Paul in Acts 17.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:21.700
Speaker 1: You know, Paul was in the city of Areopagos and he had seen so many idols, even an idol that was given to an unknown God.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:29.460
Speaker 1: And he stood there drawing examples from their culture, quoting from their poets to defend the Christian faith.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:40.740
Speaker 1: He was able to stand on two legs, applying what he saw in culture, but using it to point their heart and their mind back to the one truth, the one true God.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.500
Speaker 1: So that is the work of apologetics.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.360
Speaker 1: And I think it's important for you to understand that your faith is not blind.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.120
Speaker 1: It's not just about you just believe or.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.240
Speaker 1: Because that's what you've been taught, or that is where you.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.720
Speaker 1: That is what you've known your whole life.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.920
Speaker 1: It is reasonable and it is defendable.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:07.440
Speaker 2: Many of the people who do, who claim or who do not believe in what we believe in, and they often hold on to this because they say, well, your faith is not reasonable.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.330
Speaker 2: And some people, because they've grown up with this, God is unquestionable.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:14.410
Speaker 2: Unquestionable.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:19.410
Speaker 2: You are the Lord, you know, and you think you can't ask questions about your faith, you can't reason about your faith.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.730
Speaker 2: This is what apologetics is all about.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:32.930
Speaker 2: Like a famous apologist once said, you know, he said his mantra is helping believers think and helping thinkers believe.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:33.690
Speaker 1: Right?

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:34.050
Speaker 2: Right.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.930
Speaker 2: So that is what apologetics is all about.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:41.460
Speaker 2: So there are so many ways you can slice and dice the subject of apologetics.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:52.380
Speaker 2: There are various topics, a myriad of topics, and there are layers upon layers, but there is a foundation, and this is the beginning of the benigning.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:53.500
Speaker 2: Okay?

00:06:54.060 --> 00:07:00.940
Speaker 2: And so the foundational question we want to start with today in this series on apologetics is there a God?

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:03.420
Speaker 2: Is there a God?

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:04.170
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:12.330
Speaker 2: And the important thing is you realize that answering this question gets to shape the entirety of your life.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:18.010
Speaker 2: It determines the quality of life you're going to live here on Earth.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.330
Speaker 2: It shapes your understanding of life itself.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.010
Speaker 2: It shapes your understanding of death.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:24.170
Speaker 2: It shapes your understanding of right and wrong.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:25.210
Speaker 2: It shapes everything.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:32.260
Speaker 2: So this is the question that determines what side of the divide you're going to be on.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:33.220
Speaker 2: Okay?

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:39.380
Speaker 2: So there are three schools of thoughts when it comes to this question of whether or not there is a God.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:40.660
Speaker 2: So, pib.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:41.340
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.420
Speaker 2: Walk us through what these three schools of thoughts are.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.980
Speaker 1: All right, so the first one is atheism.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:53.140
Speaker 1: And atheism is the rejection of a belief in God or God.

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:53.540
Speaker 1: Right.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:08:03.540
Speaker 1: So some atheists, they believe that God does not exist, while some, they simply do not hold on to any belief that you know, God exists.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:04.740
Speaker 1: So that's atheism.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.380
Speaker 1: Now, the second one is agnosticism.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.620
Speaker 1: Agnosticism is the view that the existence of God or gods cannot be known.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:22.340
Speaker 1: So people like people that identify as agnostics will say, it's possible that God exists, but I don't know and it's not possible for me to know that.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.700
Speaker 2: Or they say, the evidence is not sufficient.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:25.220
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.860
Speaker 1: Or the evidence is not sufficient.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:31.310
Speaker 1: It's not conclusive enough for me to have a belief that God exists.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.630
Speaker 1: And the third one is theism.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:38.750
Speaker 1: So theism or theists are people who believe that God exists.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:48.430
Speaker 1: So this Would be like Christians, Muslims, and every other belief system that hold to the truth or the whole, to the fact that God does exist.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.230
Speaker 2: So there are two things I want to point out here.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:55.020
Speaker 2: First off, we said the starting question is there a God?

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.100
Speaker 2: That is the first gate you have to get through.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:05.220
Speaker 2: Once you land one side, hopefully you land on the side after today's class that there is a God.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.980
Speaker 2: The next question then becomes, if there is a God, which one is he?

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:12.940
Speaker 2: You see?

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:21.770
Speaker 2: So it starts to spiral out that way because like pastor Iniolua said, there are many faiths that land on the side of theism.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.090
Speaker 2: But you're on the side of theism.

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:26.650
Speaker 2: Doesn't mean you serve the same God.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.370
Speaker 2: You know, it's very important to note.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:40.490
Speaker 2: And the other thing is, you may have these three schools of thought, but when you think about it objectively, what makes the difference is the evidence.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:41.490
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:53.700
Speaker 2: Now, there are some people who say, oh, well, the agnostics may say the evidence is inconclusive, we can't really see, or is not sufficient enough for us to conclude.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:55.140
Speaker 2: But is that really the case?

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.220
Speaker 2: Is that really the case?

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:06.260
Speaker 2: There is no way you can look around you and at least observe the evidence that you have seen, the evidence that is out there.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.740
Speaker 2: Now, what can you make of it?

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:10.900
Speaker 2: I'll start with analogy.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.370
Speaker 2: Imagine you're walking into a village or you know what?

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:14.290
Speaker 2: This is a good story.

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:17.090
Speaker 2: We're in a wildfire season in Canada.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:21.010
Speaker 2: And there's this thing that happens in Canada in the summertime.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:31.090
Speaker 2: Unfortunately, there are cities that, you know, unfortunately have wildfires that consumes homes and all of that.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.250
Speaker 2: So there was this particular day, it was somewhat close to home.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:41.220
Speaker 2: We woke up like every other day and we looked out the window and everywhere was orange.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:41.740
Speaker 1: Orange?

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:42.300
Speaker 1: Yep.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:43.740
Speaker 2: Everywhere was literally orange.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:49.500
Speaker 2: You know how you take a picture in sepia tone and it feels like all those olden days pictures?

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.180
Speaker 2: Everywhere was literally orange.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.780
Speaker 2: It smelled like smoke, too, and it smelled like smoke.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:11:05.750
Speaker 2: And at that point, when you see such a sight and you smell such, your conclusion is not, oh, this smoke came out of nowhere.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:10.390
Speaker 2: Oh, well, whatever will be like, I don't know.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:16.670
Speaker 2: You know, if you're intelligent enough, you would follow and ask the right questions.

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:22.310
Speaker 2: Or if this smoke is here, you put one or two together and you realize, okay, there is no smoke without fire.

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:23.830
Speaker 2: There is smoke around.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.830
Speaker 2: There must have been fire that brought about the smoke.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:29.640
Speaker 2: And this is the way to think.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.000
Speaker 2: Follow the evidence.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.120
Speaker 2: If there are things that exist in a certain way, how do we have them to be.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:43.880
Speaker 2: And so when we ask about whether we can prove the existence of God or not, follow the evidence.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.800
Speaker 2: Okay, and this is something that we have coined.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:48.520
Speaker 2: Well, not we.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:52.040
Speaker 2: In theological circles, it's called natural theology.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:12:02.320
Speaker 2: Are there things we can observe in nature that explains to us and can convince us that there is indeed a God who exists?

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.520
Speaker 2: Do you want to expand a little bit on the natural theology?

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:05.240
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:14.240
Speaker 1: And I think another example that even comes to mind on that phenomenon when you think of natural theology is observing the things around you.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:20.680
Speaker 1: Say, for example, a caveman was just walking in the forest and he saw a rock, and then he kicked that rock.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:22.600
Speaker 1: He didn't put the rug there.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:28.160
Speaker 1: But that would have triggered something in his mind to say, okay, this rock could not have been there by chance.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:39.200
Speaker 1: It's the same way if, say, for example, he walked a bit further and he saw a wristwatch in his mind, what would start to happen is, how did this wristwatch get here?

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.640
Speaker 1: How was this wristwatch designed?

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:41.760
Speaker 1: Who made the leather?

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:45.200
Speaker 1: Who made the hands, you know, in the clock and making it tick?

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:46.760
Speaker 1: So that tells you that for.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:51.790
Speaker 1: For everything that we see in our natural environment has a reason.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:53.470
Speaker 1: And that's because of.

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:53.910
Speaker 1: Because of.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:54.270
Speaker 1: We can.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:54.830
Speaker 1: Because we can.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:57.710
Speaker 1: Because we can observe our natural environment.

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:00.270
Speaker 1: That tells us that it did not just happen by chance.

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.750
Speaker 1: It points to something bigger.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:04.270
Speaker 2: That's beautiful.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.830
Speaker 2: So if I was to tie scriptures to this.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:10.430
Speaker 2: Romans chapter 1, Romans chapter 1 and verse 19.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:14.300
Speaker 2: He says, for what can be known about God is plain to.

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:16.850
Speaker 2: To them because God has shown it to them.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:17.810
Speaker 1: It's.

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:19.090
Speaker 2: He's.

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:23.810
Speaker 2: This is a blatant call to those who claim to be skeptics or agnostics.

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:27.650
Speaker 2: He said, if you want to know anything about God has made it plain.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:30.290
Speaker 2: Yeah, God has made it plain to you.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:33.090
Speaker 2: So the evidence is in plain sight.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.050
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:35.410
Speaker 2: There's nothing to hide here.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:39.830
Speaker 2: See, for his invisible attributes, namely his eternal power and.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:47.340
Speaker 2: And his divine nature have been clearly perceived ever since the creation of the world in the things that had been made.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:49.580
Speaker 2: So they are without excuse.

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.380
Speaker 2: So he's telling you that you can observe nature.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:52.940
Speaker 1: Right.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.020
Speaker 2: You can observe natural creation.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.340
Speaker 2: And that gives you an idea.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:03.100
Speaker 2: It gives you an incentive to understanding the invisible creator.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.540
Speaker 2: The creation points you to the creator.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:07.600
Speaker 2: Right, Right.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.240
Speaker 2: So this is what natural theology is.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:15.120
Speaker 2: And he's basically telling you that all this evidence has been there from day one.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:15.600
Speaker 1: Right.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.680
Speaker 2: Think about the radio broadcasting.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:25.600
Speaker 2: The fact that you don't see the signals of the broadcast does not mean that the signals are not there.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:32.660
Speaker 2: You have to intentionally whip out your antenna and rightly decipher the signals and.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:34.490
Speaker 2: And arrive at the right conclusion.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:37.770
Speaker 2: So when you look at nature, what do you see?
